=====Usage notes=====
{{anchor|en|gay noun notes}}
* Used as a noun denoting a person ({{senseno|en|Q101922089}}), ''gay'' may be regarded as offensive when referring to particular individuals.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20070125105128/http://www.bartleby.com/64/C006/034.html The American Heritage® Book of English Usage: A Practical and Authoritative Guide to Contemporary English] (1996), "gay"</ref>
* ''Gay'' is sometimes used broadly to refer to any man who is attracted to and/or sexually active with other men, or any woman attracted to or active with other women, even if not exclusively, e.g. if their orientation is in fact [[bisexual]].<!--see e.g. a Google Books search for "bisexual gays"--><ref>For example: David Kaufman, ''Untying the Knot: A Husband and Wife's Story of Coming Out Together'' (2012, {{ISBN|1936374900}}): '''Gays''', and apparently lesbians, are discouraged from being openly bisexual. The cultural standard in the gay community is that you have to pick one sex and stick to it.</ref> The word {{m|en|queer}} has likewise had various meanings.

